\subsection{Schema Confusability Score}
\label{sec:scs}

We define the \emph{Schema Confusability Score} (SCS) between types $t_i$ and $t_j$ as the cosine similarity of their embeddings:
%
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{SCS}(t_i, t_j) = \cos\bigl(\phi(t_i),\, \phi(t_j)\bigr)
  \label{eq:scs}
\end{equation}
%
where $\phi$ maps type names to dense vectors.
For a batch $B \subseteq \mathcal{S}$ of types drawn from schema~$\mathcal{S}$, the \emph{Batch Confusability Score} aggregates pairwise confusability:
%
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{BCS}(B) = \frac{2}{|B|\,(|B|-1)} \sum_{\substack{t_i, t_j \in B \\ i < j}} \mathrm{SCS}(t_i, t_j)
  \label{eq:bcs}
\end{equation}
%

We compute $\phi$ with two models:
(1)~\textbf{Qwen3-32B}, the extraction model itself, using the last-token hidden state after encoding the type name (dimension 5{,}120); and
(2)~\textbf{all-MiniLM-L6-v2} \citep{TODO-reimers-gurevych-2019}, a general-purpose sentence encoder (dimension 384).
This dual-embedding design enables analysis of whether extraction-model-aligned representations provide better confusability proxies than task-agnostic alternatives (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\subsection{Experimental Design}
\label{sec:design}

We design two complementary experiments to disentangle the effects of type count and type confusability.

\paragraph{Experiment~1: Count Curve.}
For each dataset, we vary the number of types presented in a single prompt from 1 (solo extraction, where each type is evaluated independently) to the full schema size.
At each count level above~1, we sample 5 random type batches.
All batches are evaluated on the same fixed instance set (1{,}000 instances for NER and RE; the full validation set for ED).
This establishes the count--degradation baseline and characterizes how BCS naturally covaries with count.

\paragraph{Experiment~2: BCS-Controlled.}
To isolate confusability from count, we hold the type count fixed and construct batches that differ maximally in BCS.
For each count level, we select \emph{low-BCS} batches (types with minimal pairwise similarity) and \emph{high-BCS} batches (types with maximal pairwise similarity), alongside random baselines.
On Few-NERD, we evaluate at counts $\{6, 12\}$ with 27~batches per count level (25~random, 1~low-BCS extreme, 1~high-BCS extreme; $n{=}54$ total).
On FewRel, we evaluate at counts $\{8, 16\}$ over all 80~relations.
Any performance gap between low-BCS and high-BCS conditions at the same count is attributable to type confusability, not type count.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

We adopt task-appropriate metrics reflecting the distinct output structures of NER, RE, and ED.

\paragraph{NER (Few-NERD).}
Entity-level F1 with text matching: a predicted entity $(m, t)$ is a true positive if the gold annotations for the same input contain an entity with identical mention text~$m$ and type~$t$.
This accommodates the text-based (rather than span-indexed) output format of LLM extraction.
We additionally decompose into precision and recall to distinguish count effects (recall decline) from confusability effects (type confusion, reflected in precision).

\paragraph{RE (FewRel).}
Accuracy: each instance requires predicting one relation from the presented schema for a given entity pair.

\paragraph{ED (MAVEN).}
Relaxed F1.
A predicted trigger $(p_{\text{text}}, p_{\text{type}})$ is a true positive if there exists a gold annotation $(g_{\text{text}}, g_{\text{type}})$ satisfying two conditions:
(1)~$p_{\text{type}} = g_{\text{type}}$ (exact event type match), and
(2)~$\mathrm{tokens}(p_{\text{text}}) \cap \mathrm{tokens}(g_{\text{text}}) \neq \emptyset$ (at least one shared token between predicted and gold trigger spans).
This relaxation is necessary because exact trigger matching yields F1~${<}\,0.04$ due to inherent boundary ambiguity in event trigger annotation.

\paragraph{Uncertainty Quantification.}
All key correlations and effect sizes are accompanied by 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals (1{,}000 resamples, percentile method).

\subsection{Model and Inference}
\label{sec:model}

We use \textbf{Qwen3-32B} with FP8 quantization as the primary extraction model, served via vLLM~\citep{TODO-kwon-vllm-2023}.
The model receives a structured prompt containing: (1)~the input text, (2)~the target type list, and (3)~task-specific extraction instructions requesting JSON-formatted output.
We use greedy decoding (temperature~$= 0$) with the model's thinking mode disabled to ensure deterministic, concise outputs.
Maximum context length is set to 4{,}096 tokens.
Full prompt templates are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}.
